  "narrative": "Special Note: ***Calves weaned over 45 days are left untagged*** Compared to last sale (11-03-26): Steers under 450 lbs were too lightly tested either this sale or last sale to develop any market trend. The best test for feeder steers was for 450-599 lbs steers which sold mostly steady to 10.00 lower. All other weights of steers were too lightly tested last week to develop an accurate market trend, however mostly lower undertones were noticed. One load lot of reputation steers weighing 656 lbs topped the market at 396.50 defying market direction. Heifer calves were all too lightly tested last sale to develop an accurate market trend, however higher undertones were noticed on all offerings. Quality this week was mostly average to very attractive. Several reputation ranchers sold calves this week, offering buyers a chance at all of their 2026 calf crop. These offerings sold on very good demand for all weights. Many cutback calves continue to be seen as well and these offerings were met with mostly moderate demand. Demand for cattle suitable to run on grass or wheat was very good. Demand for this class improved as buyers pushed to fill loads, especially late in the sale. Demand for replacement heifers was very good as several high quality offerings were seen this week. Grass buyers and replacement buyers both showed very good demand for offerings and pushed the entire heifer complex higher. CME positions sold off sharply last week, but rebounded sharply for the first two trading days this week. Market activity this week was mostly active with a large pool of buyers noticed both ringside and over the internet. Weigh up cows sold on mostly light to moderate demand for moderate to heavy offerings. Feeding cow demand was mostly light to moderate this week. Feeding cows sold mostly 5.00-10.00 lower on all classes. Canadian buyers were back in the market this week, these buyers quickly filled loads and exited the market. Feeding cows softened as the sale progressed. Slaughter cows sold mostly steady to weak on all classes. Cows offered buyers very good weigh conditions this sale. Slaughter bulls sold generally steady in a narrow comparison. Young age cows sold on good to very good demand this week. Quality was mostly average this week. These offerings sold mostly 5.00-10.00 lower on a lower quality offering. Supply included: 74% Feeder Cattle (58% Steers, 41% Heifers, 1% Bulls); 19% Slaughter Cattle (96% Cows, 4% Bulls); 8% Replacement Cattle (93% Stock Cows, 7% Bred Cows). Feeder cattle supply over 600 lbs was 29%.",
